WEBVTT

00:00:05.315 --> 00:00:07.025
Hm? Huh?

00:00:07.025 --> 00:00:09.486
I'm gonna live forever!

00:00:09.486 --> 00:00:11.070
[PETER & BENJAMIN GASP]

00:00:13.114 --> 00:00:14.282
Oh...

00:00:14.282 --> 00:00:16.409
Told you!

00:00:19.454 --> 00:00:21.289
[ALL YELL] [LAUGHING]

00:00:21.289 --> 00:00:23.500
[♪♪♪]

00:00:26.503 --> 00:00:27.754
[COTTON-TAIL LAUGHING]

00:00:27.754 --> 00:00:29.673
Thank you.

00:00:32.175 --> 00:00:35.470
Do you know what happens after you eat too much sugar?

00:00:36.429 --> 00:00:37.889
That.

00:00:37.889 --> 00:00:39.641
Come on, sweeties.

00:00:43.729 --> 00:00:45.772
Please come in. Oh.

00:00:47.315 --> 00:00:48.817
There they are.

00:00:48.817 --> 00:00:50.068
Hello. Bea.

00:00:50.068 --> 00:00:51.611
You're even lovelier

00:00:51.611 --> 00:00:53.655
than I imagined. Oh!

00:00:53.655 --> 00:00:54.823
Thomas. Hello.

00:00:54.823 --> 00:00:56.241
Handsome devil. Well...

00:00:56.241 --> 00:00:58.577
I'm sure I'm one of many beating down your door,

00:00:58.577 --> 00:00:59.994
begging to publish your next book.

00:00:59.994 --> 00:01:02.163
Yes, well, so many.

00:01:02.163 --> 00:01:04.332
In fact, we don't have a door anymore.

00:01:04.332 --> 00:01:06.084
It's been beaten off its hinges.

00:01:06.084 --> 00:01:07.711
[ALL LAUGHING]

00:01:07.711 --> 00:01:09.337
And the stars themselves,

00:01:09.337 --> 00:01:11.590
with Peter, the ringleader.

00:01:11.590 --> 00:01:13.842
Always out front. Sweet.

00:01:13.842 --> 00:01:16.678
I'm not your leader. I'm not always out front.

00:01:16.678 --> 00:01:18.971
You come here. Benjamin, swing out a little.

00:01:18.971 --> 00:01:21.892
Mops, back it up to your right. Flopsy, you come this way.

00:01:21.892 --> 00:01:23.727
Benjamin, Mops, split the difference.

00:01:23.727 --> 00:01:27.146
There you go. You'll slot here. I'll go here, and...

00:01:27.146 --> 00:01:28.774
[GROANS]

00:01:28.774 --> 00:01:30.191
If you'll allow me to gush,

00:01:30.191 --> 00:01:33.069
the book is absolutely exquisite.

00:01:33.069 --> 00:01:36.448
A triumph. Oh, I'll allow you. Heh.

00:01:36.448 --> 00:01:40.243
"What greater gift than the love of a rabbit."

00:01:42.829 --> 00:01:44.539
Charles Dickens. Yes.

00:01:44.539 --> 00:01:47.166
Well, he said "a cat." You obviously knew that.

00:01:47.166 --> 00:01:48.585
Of course. I love Dickens.

00:01:48.585 --> 00:01:50.211
Know every word he's written. You do?

00:01:50.211 --> 00:01:51.421
Mm-hm. No, you don't.

00:01:51.421 --> 00:01:53.131
I do. I don't think so.

00:01:53.131 --> 00:01:54.633
[ALL LAUGH]

00:01:54.633 --> 00:01:55.926
Strong. Yeah.

00:01:55.926 --> 00:01:57.302
You box? Yeah, no, I...

00:01:57.302 --> 00:01:58.470
Yeah, I do. I do box.

00:01:58.470 --> 00:01:59.721
You do? Yep.

00:01:59.721 --> 00:02:00.972
No, you don't. Yes, I do.

00:02:00.972 --> 00:02:03.182
When? I flop the heavy ropes.

00:02:03.182 --> 00:02:06.645
I move the heavy metal bars. I've got those balls of sand.

00:02:06.645 --> 00:02:09.731
I do push-ups outside in the thing with my shirt off.

00:02:09.731 --> 00:02:12.317
And I also like protein ointments.

00:02:12.317 --> 00:02:14.193
We should get in the ring sometime.

00:02:14.193 --> 00:02:15.570
Yeah. That's not a problem.

00:02:15.570 --> 00:02:17.071
Now, I'd like to walk you... Sorry.

00:02:17.071 --> 00:02:18.364
...through our strategy.

00:02:18.364 --> 00:02:22.118
Carlos, some water for our rabbits.

00:02:22.118 --> 00:02:23.829
Bea, sit right here.

00:02:23.829 --> 00:02:26.498
Sparkling and still.

00:02:26.498 --> 00:02:27.749
[BOTH GASP]

00:02:27.749 --> 00:02:31.878
Wow! Ooh, fancy.

00:02:31.878 --> 00:02:33.839
[BOTH SLURPING, GAGGING]

00:02:33.839 --> 00:02:35.757
It's like drinking sand.

00:02:35.757 --> 00:02:37.926
I like it. It's delicious.

00:02:37.926 --> 00:02:39.469
You're saying that to be different.

00:02:39.469 --> 00:02:42.054
Am not. Drink some more, then.

00:02:44.098 --> 00:02:46.893
[INHALES SHARPLY] [GASPS]

00:02:46.893 --> 00:02:49.562
Blech, it's amazing.

00:02:49.562 --> 00:02:52.398
[GASPS] [BELCHES LOUDLY]

00:02:52.398 --> 00:02:53.525
[GROANS]

00:02:53.525 --> 00:02:55.234
So we want to start by printing

00:02:55.234 --> 00:02:59.447
5000 copies of your book.

00:02:59.447 --> 00:03:00.699
Wow. Five thousand?

00:03:00.699 --> 00:03:02.116
I assume you want to write

00:03:02.116 --> 00:03:03.451
a second one?

00:03:03.451 --> 00:03:06.955
I hadn't really thought about it, but...

00:03:06.955 --> 00:03:09.415
I don't know, I guess I do have a brief framework

00:03:09.415 --> 00:03:11.250
for a 23-book series

00:03:11.250 --> 00:03:14.587
featuring 109 characters based on the animals in my life,

00:03:14.587 --> 00:03:17.507
creating a narrative about morality, nature and family,

00:03:17.507 --> 00:03:20.259
set in the fields and towns around our farm.

00:03:20.259 --> 00:03:21.845
Fantastic.

00:03:21.845 --> 00:03:25.264
Fantastic. Fantastic.

00:03:25.264 --> 00:03:28.601
Because it's in your next book that we see huge potential.

00:03:28.601 --> 00:03:30.353
Fantastic. Fantastic.

00:03:30.353 --> 00:03:32.063
Fantastic. Fantastic.

00:03:32.063 --> 00:03:33.356
I don't trust this guy.

00:03:33.356 --> 00:03:36.651
We've done some research on your book.

00:03:36.651 --> 00:03:40.030
Now, people liked the story and the setting,

00:03:40.030 --> 00:03:43.241
but what they really loved were the rabbits.

00:03:43.241 --> 00:03:47.037
In the second book, we want to emphasize their individuality.

00:03:48.287 --> 00:03:50.916
"Benjamin, the wise one."

00:03:50.916 --> 00:03:53.001
"Cotton-Tail, the firecracker."

00:03:53.001 --> 00:03:54.419
This guy is good.

00:03:54.419 --> 00:03:57.047
"Flopsy and Mopsy, the dynamic duo."

00:03:57.047 --> 00:03:59.507
Why are we always treated...? As a unit?

00:03:59.507 --> 00:04:01.342
But we do... Look good.

00:04:01.342 --> 00:04:03.720
And then, finally, Peter. What?

00:04:03.720 --> 00:04:06.222
I get one? This is crazy.

00:04:06.222 --> 00:04:07.849
We have two options for him.

00:04:07.849 --> 00:04:10.602
I get two?

00:04:10.602 --> 00:04:12.854
"The mischief maker." Or... Mm.

00:04:14.564 --> 00:04:16.524
[IN DEEP VOICE] "The bad seed."

00:04:16.524 --> 00:04:19.027
Nope. And he was doing so well too.

00:04:19.027 --> 00:04:20.946
That one. That's the one. You've nailed it.

00:04:20.946 --> 00:04:22.614
What? It's our favorite too.

00:04:22.614 --> 00:04:24.657
Really speaks to his character. No, it doesn't.

00:04:24.657 --> 00:04:27.410
"The bad seed" seems harsh, don't you think?

00:04:27.410 --> 00:04:29.537
Well, he is a bit of a brat, really.

00:04:29.537 --> 00:04:31.081
Even according to your own book,

00:04:31.081 --> 00:04:33.666
he nearly broke you two up and destroyed his family.

00:04:33.666 --> 00:04:36.419
He's a little mischievous, but he doesn't mean anything by it.

00:04:36.419 --> 00:04:38.880
Exactly. Thank you. I did catch him yesterday

00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:40.590
trying to steal one of my tomatoes.

00:04:40.590 --> 00:04:43.635
No, no, no. I was making sure no one stole your tomatoes.

00:04:43.635 --> 00:04:45.762
I also imagine his voice to be annoying.

00:04:45.762 --> 00:04:47.639
[HIGH-PITCHED] What?!

00:04:47.639 --> 00:04:51.309
My voice isn't annoying, right?

00:04:52.268 --> 00:04:54.270
I have something to show you.

00:04:55.563 --> 00:04:58.483
Here's how much we want to do this with you.

00:04:58.483 --> 00:04:59.692
[GASPS]

00:04:59.692 --> 00:05:01.277
BEA: Oh, my goodness.

00:05:01.277 --> 00:05:02.654
[NIGEL & THOMAS CHUCKLING]

00:05:02.654 --> 00:05:04.114
COTTON-TAIL, MOPSY & FLOPSY: Whoa!

00:05:04.114 --> 00:05:05.740
My ears are gigantic.

00:05:05.740 --> 00:05:07.909
Peter really looks like a villain.

00:05:07.909 --> 00:05:10.662
NIGEL: Every story needs one. I'm not a villain.

00:05:10.662 --> 00:05:12.998
That's not an accurate reflection of any of us.

00:05:12.998 --> 00:05:15.166
It's like I'm looking in a mirror.

00:05:15.166 --> 00:05:17.002
BEA: I love it. What?

00:05:17.002 --> 00:05:19.129
I think it's terrific.

00:05:19.129 --> 00:05:21.506
Oh, it's incredible.

00:05:21.506 --> 00:05:24.425
NIGEL: It must be so precious to see your family up there.

00:05:24.425 --> 00:05:26.761
BEA: It's my dream come true.

00:05:26.761 --> 00:05:29.388
[CONVERSATION CONTINUES INDISTINCTLY]

00:05:29.388 --> 00:05:30.431
[SIGHS]

00:05:30.431 --> 00:05:33.476
BEA: They look amazing!

00:05:33.476 --> 00:05:36.228
♪ I walk a lonely road ♪

00:05:36.228 --> 00:05:39.983
♪ The only one That I have ever known ♪

00:05:39.983 --> 00:05:41.693
♪ Don't know where it goes ♪

00:05:41.693 --> 00:05:42.735
[GROANS]

00:05:42.735 --> 00:05:43.862
♪ But it's home to me ♪

00:05:43.862 --> 00:05:45.738
♪ And I walk alone ♪

